---
pagination:
  data: collections.versionChainCurrent
  size: 1
  alias: item
permalink: "{{ item.data.version_of }}latest/"
eleventyExcludeFromCollections: true
---
{#-
  THE `latest/` ALIAS for a versioned page.

  Policy, 12 August 2026: a locked page is superseded rather than edited, and
  each version keeps its own permanent URL — /lore/foo/, then /lore/foo-2/ —
  so that a citation always resolves to the text that was cited. That is the
  whole point, and it is why the ORIGINAL is never redirected.

  But it leaves a reader who simply wants the current text with no address to
  ask for, and no way to link to "whatever this has become". This is that
  address: /lore/foo/latest/ redirects to whichever version is current.

  SO THE TWO AUDIENCES GET DIFFERENT URLS, ON PURPOSE.
    /lore/foo/         what was cited. Frozen. Carries a note pointing forward.
    /lore/foo-2/       the current text, at its own permanent address.
    /lore/foo/latest/  "the newest, whatever it is." Never frozen.

  A citation uses the first. Internal navigation and anyone tracking the work
  uses the third. Neither pretends to be the other, which is the failure mode
  of the common arrangement where the original URL silently starts serving new
  content.

  HOW "CURRENT" IS DECIDED: a page in a chain declares `version_of` (the
  original's URL). The one with no `superseded_by` is the current version, and
  there must be exactly one — scripts/validate-content.js fails the build
  otherwise, because two current versions in a chain means someone forgot a
  forward note and the alias would be a coin toss.

  NO ALIAS EXISTS UNTIL A CHAIN DOES. A page nobody has superseded is already
  current at its own URL, so /lore/foo/latest/ appears the moment /lore/foo-2/
  does. Documented rather than worked around: generating an alias for every page
  on the site would be ~500 stubs earning nothing.

  Static stub rather than a server redirect, canonical + noindex, for exactly
  the reasons src/chapter-aliases.njk gives at length: .htaccess does not exist
  on GitHub Pages and this repo deploys to both, and an alias must never compete
  with the page it points at.
-#}
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>{{ item.data.title }} — latest version</title>
<link rel="canonical" href="/star-rangers{{ item.url }}">
<meta name="robots" content="noindex, follow">
<meta http-equiv="refresh" content="0; url=/star-rangers{{ item.url }}">
<style>
  :root { color-scheme: dark light; }
  body {
    margin: 0; min-height: 100vh; display: grid; place-items: center;
    padding: 2rem; background: #12151b; color: #e8e6e1;
    font-family: Georgia, "Times New Roman", serif; line-height: 1.6;
  }
  main { max-width: 34rem; text-align: center; }
  h1 { font-size: 1.5rem; font-weight: normal; margin: 0 0 1rem; }
  p { margin: 0 0 1rem; }
  a { color: #9db8d8; }
  .note { font-size: 0.9rem; opacity: 0.7; }
</style>
</head>
<body>
<main>
  <h1>{{ item.data.title }}</h1>
  <p>This address always points at the <strong>current</strong> version of this
    entry. It is the link to use when you want the newest text rather than a
    particular one.</p>
  <p>If you meant to cite a specific version, link to that version's own
    address instead — it will not change under you.</p>
  <p><a href="/star-rangers{{ item.url }}">Continue to the current version</a></p>
  <p class="note">If you are not redirected automatically, follow the link above.</p>
</main>
</body>
</html>
